core_fund_growth":0.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.0,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":false,"_score_shrhldr_yield":11.1,"_gate_share_shrink":0.15195067014622854,"_gp_share_shrink":false,"_score_share_shrink":0.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":4.065060240963855,"_gp_sbc_dilution":false,"_score_sbc_dilution":0.0,"_gate_insider_own":0.01574,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":10.5,"_gate_insider_buying":0.0,"_gp_insider_buying":false,"_score_insider_buying":0.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"Relay Therapeutics, Inc. operates as a clinical-stage precision medicines company. It transforms the drug discovery process with a focus on enhancing small molecule therapeutic discovery in targeted oncology and genetic disease indications. The company's lead product candidates include RLY-2608, a pan-mutant and isoform-selective phosphoinostide 3 kinase alpha, or PI3Ka, inhibitor that is in clinical development for the treatment of breast cancer, solid tumor, and vascular malformations; and aGal chaperone for Fabry diseases. It also develops RLY-8161, an oncogene driver that belongs to the RAS family of signaling proteins. In addition, the company develops lirafugratinib (RLY-4008), a receptor tyrosine kinase for the treatment of cancer. It has collaboration and license agreements with D. E. Shaw Research, LLC to research certain biological targets through the use of D. E. Shaw Research computational modeling capabilities focused on analysis of protein motion to develop and commercialize compounds and products directed to such targets; Elevar Therapeutics, Inc. for the development and commercialization of RLY-4008; and Pfizer Inc. for the development of RLY-2608 in combination with fulvestrant and atirmociclib. The company was formerly known as Allostery, Inc. and changed its name to Relay Therapeutics, Inc. in December 2015. Relay Therapeutics, Inc. was incorporated in 2015 and is headquartered in Cambridge, Massachusetts.","ceo_bio":"Dr. Sanjiv K. Patel M.A., M.B.A., M.D., MBBS | CEO, President & Director | Age 51 | Compensation: $1.1M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 202 people. Revenue per employee of $76K ranks in the bottom quartile among Healthcare peers, declining at 29%/yr \u2014 weakening productivity. Stock-based compensation of $309K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 15\u00d7 revenue per employee (reasonable relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (9/10).","financial_summary":["Relay Therapeutics, Inc. is a mid-cap healthcare business with a $4.1B market cap on $15M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +123% over the past three years, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","Returns on capital run at -53% against a 12% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The company is burning through $236M of cash, and without positive owner earnings any valuation rests on hope rather than evidence.","On the balance sheet, interest coverage of 40\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Shareholder yield is only 0.0%, a thin return of capital, and stock-based comp at 407% of revenue is silent dilution an owner has to account for, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and profit capture is only 0.0\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Piotroski score 3/9 \u2014 the fundamentals are deteriorating on multiple fronts","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Very elevated short interest (23.4% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting against the business","sev":"red","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Operating margins 1972% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage the moat cannot hide","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"